    /**
     * @class Frontend_Endpoints
     * @since 2.8.6
     */
    class Frontend_Endpoints
    {
        /**
         * Dispatch the current frontend endpoint action.
         *
         * @return void
         */
        public static function handle()
        {
        }
        /**
         * Redirect legacy unsubscribe links to the communication page.
         *
         * A request resolves a customer only from an `aw_token` or, behind the
         * `automatewoo/preferences/accept_customer_key` filter, a `customer_key` it
         * already carries. The historic `user` (email) parameter is intentionally
         * no longer honoured: resolving a customer from a bare email address turned
         * any visitor's knowledge of that address into an unauthenticated
         * disclosure of the customer's persistent `customer_key` — the bearer
         * credential for the communication-preferences surface — straight out of
         * the redirect `Location` header. An email address is an identifier, not an
         * authenticator, so it must not resolve (or silently create) a customer
         * here. Genuine unsubscribe links generated by AutomateWoo carry the
         * `aw_token`, so the legitimate flow is unaffected.
         *
         * An `aw_token` resolves a customer here only when its own bound intent is
         * `unsubscribe` (expired or not, per rule R1) — never based on this
         * endpoint's action or on an `intent` query arg. A token bound to any
         * other intent (a management or `tracking-opt-out` token, say) resolves
         * nothing: the redirect this endpoint builds performs a full marketing
         * opt-out, an action such a token was never bound to, and it would also
         * come back as a freshly minted, live credential from the redirect.
         *
         * A token-authorised request is redirected with the very token that
         * authorised it, never a freshly minted one. Minting here would upgrade the
         * presented credential: a fresh `unsubscribe` token never expires and is
         * signed under the current site version, so a token accepted only under the
         * rotation grace would come back as a credential that outlives the rotation
         * indefinitely — while the rotation tool promises existing links stop
         * working. The presented token is enough to complete the unsubscribe on the
         * communication page, which honours the same grace and rule R1, and it keeps
         * exactly its own remaining lifetime — no more.
         *
         * @since 4.0.0
         * @since 6.8.2 The legacy `user` (email) parameter is no longer resolved.
         * @since 6.9.1 Accepts `aw_token` before falling back to `customer_key`, and
         *              passes an accepted token through instead of minting a new one.
         * @since 6.9.1 An authentic token bound to a non-`unsubscribe` intent, with no
         *              other accepted credential, redirects with a state describing what
         *              happened (`link-expired`, or `link-not-for-unsubscribe` for a live
         *              one) instead of the generic no-customer state, which told the
         *              holder of a valid credential their account may have been deleted.
         *              A raw `customer_key` presented after key acceptance is switched
         *              off redirects with `link-expired` for the same reason, the
         *              `workflow` request arg survives the key-acceptance filter being
         *              off, and a key redemption is counted only once the redirect that
         *              accepts it is actually issued.
         */
        public static function catch_legacy_unsubscribe_url()
        {
        }
        /**
         * Builds the communication-page unsubscribe redirect carrying the token the
         * visitor presented, mirroring the query args
